#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/heterogeneous.md:1
msgid "Heterogeneous Collections with Enums"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/heterogeneous.md:3
msgid ""
"Enums give us a way to create collections that can store different types of "
"element at runtime:"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/heterogeneous.md:27
msgid "\"Fido\""
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/heterogeneous.md:36
msgid ""
"A common situation where we might need dynamic polymorphism is when we want "
"to store different types of value in the same collection."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/heterogeneous.md:39
msgid ""
"In the above example, we want to store both `Cat`s and `Dog`s in the same "
"list. `Vec` doesn't support this directly: All elements of the `Vec` must be "
"the same type."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/heterogeneous.md:43
msgid ""
"Wrapping our two different pet types into a single `AnyPet` enum gives us a "
"unified type representation that can be stored in a `Vec`, while allowing "
"individual elements of the `Vec` to be different types."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/heterogeneous.md:47
msgid ""
"This requires that we know all possible pet types up front, as we need to "
"explicitly list them as different variants of the `AnyPet` enum. This works "
"well for libraries or applications that define the full set of possible "
"types, but does not allow downstream users to extend our list of pet types."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/enums/heterogeneous.md:52
msgid ""
"Later we will see that we can do the same thing with `dyn`, which allows "
"downstream extension at the cost of being harder to downcast and requiring "
"dynamic dispatch."
msgstr ""
